      {
         "id": "SECTION_prerequisites",
         "text": [
            {
               "text": "<p><span id=\"htZoomImageMsg\"></span></p>",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<p><h3>Prerequisites</h3>This is the last text of the first chapter. To understand where numbers can be found, we recommend reading all the texts before this one:</p><p><ul><li><b>Our sides are equal</b>: in this first text, we show that our body has two equal sides (<htcite0>).</li><li><b>Counting</b>: we introduce the idea of counting using our body (<htcite1>).</li><li><b>Beyond fingers</b>: we explore the limitations of counting with our hands and the need for new tools (<htcite2>).</li><li><b>Drawing to count</b>: we show how we change from our fingers to geometric pictures (<htcite3>).</li><li><b>Our current numbers</b>: we present the numbers we use today (<htcite4>).</li><li><b>Building to count (Soroban, 算盤)</b>: we learn how Japan (Nippon-koku or Nihon-koku, 日本国) built its abacus and how to use it to count (<htcite5>).</li><li><b>Building to count (Suanpan, 算盤)</b>: we learn about the abacus built in China (Zhōngguó, 中国) and how to represent numbers on it (<htcite6>).</li><li><b>Translation game (Part I)</b>: the first phase of this game, which trains numbers between 1 and 9 (<htcite7>).</li><li><b>Overcoming limits</b>: we present the logic behind the sequence of numbers from 0 to 19 (<htcite8>).</li><li><b>Hands and music</b>: with the help of the music produced by our hands, we reinforce counting from 0 to 10 (<htcite9>).</li><li><b>Numerical families (Part I)</b>: we study the tens, along with the <i>ajawob</i> dynasty that ruled the city of Copán, Honduras (<htcite10>).</li><li><b>Change of direction (Part I)</b>: we study the logic behind the different approach used by indigenous communities as their numbers grow (<htcite11>).</li><li><b>Walking and singing</b>: we study who was the first person in the family to walk and its relationship to indigenous numbers (<htcite12>).</li><li><b>Numerical families (Part II)</b>: we continue exploring the numerical orders up to the billions (<htcite13>).</li><li><b>Change of direction (Part II)</b>: we explore how indigenous peoples represent large numbers (<htcite14>).</li><li><b>Higher orders with Soroban and Suanpan (算盤)</b>: we learn how to represent on the abacuses the numerical orders studied in previous texts (<htcite15>).</li><li><b>Schyoty (счёты), or the vertical abacus</b>: we study the vertical abacus (<htcite16>).</li><li><b>Natural limits</b>: we explore various natural limits and how mathematics is used to describe them (<htcite17>).</li></ul></p>",
