      {
         "id": "SECTION_ages",
         "text": [
            {
               "text": "<p><h3>Ages</h3>Today we have scientific tools to measure how old things are - from bones to mountains to the entire universe. But for most of human history, people did not have these tools. Does that mean they did not think about the past? Not at all.</p><p>Ancient peoples told stories about their origins, using numbers that were more poetic than precise. For example, the Sumerian King List - an ancient document from Mesopotamia that mixes real kings with mythical ones - says that 8 kings ruled for a total of 241,200 years, with one king alone ruling for 43,200 years (<htcite0> ; <htcite1> ; <htcite2>).</p>",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 0,
                     "uuid": "cb216fdf-1282-42e0-aaf4-3547b53857d7",
                     "text": "Black, J.A. et al.",
                     "page": "",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "1998",
                        "month": "04",
                        "day": "16"
                     }
                  },
                  {
                     "type": 0,
                     "uuid": "c8dd380c-6f66-415c-94fa-b116308adeef",
                     "text": "Black, J.A. et al.",
                     "page": "",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "1998",
                        "month": "04",
                        "day": "16"
                     }
                  },
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "55e5065b-f9c7-4a51-a1bd-bf2df0f1fb2a",
                     "text": "History Tracers Team",
                     "page": "Sumerians - The Mythological Kings",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "",
                        "month": "",
                        "day": ""
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<p class=\"desc\"><img id=\"imgKingList\" onclick=\"htImageZoom('imgKingList', '0%')\" class=\"imgcenter\" style=\"width: 30%;\" /><b>Figure 3</b>: Sumerian King List. © Ashmolean Museum, University of Oxford (<htcite0>).</p>",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "55e5065b-f9c7-4a51-a1bd-bf2df0f1fb2a",
                     "text": "History Tracers Team",
                     "page": "Sumerians - The Mythological Kings",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "",
                        "month": "",
                        "day": ""
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<p>The Sumerian King List attributes periods of rule of millennia - more than a thousand years - to some people. As we read further in ancient texts, we notice something interesting: the reigns gradually get shorter, from thousands of years to centuries - periods of more than 100 years - and, finally, they reach the decades we know today (<htcite0>; <htcite1>; <htcite2>).</p>",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 0,
                     "uuid": "cb216fdf-1282-42e0-aaf4-3547b53857d7",
                     "text": "Black, J.A. et al.",
                     "page": "",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "1998",
                        "month": "04",
                        "day": "16"
                     }
                  },
                  {
                     "type": 0,
                     "uuid": "c8dd380c-6f66-415c-94fa-b116308adeef",
                     "text": "Black, J.A. et al.",
                     "page": "",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "1998",
                        "month": "04",
                        "day": "16"
                     }
                  },
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "55e5065b-f9c7-4a51-a1bd-bf2df0f1fb2a",
                     "text": "History Tracers Team",
                     "page": "Sumerians - The Mythological Kings",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "",
                        "month": "",
                        "day": ""
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<p><center><div class=\"first_steps_reflection_parent\"><div class=\"first_steps_reflection question_mark\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-brain\"></i></div><div class=\"first_steps_reflection\"><b>Reflection 1: Long life</b><br /><br />Would it be possible for people to have lived for centuries or millennia, as the Sumerians wrote and other peoples possibly learned from them?</div></div></center></p>",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            }
         ]
      },
      {
         "id": "SECTION_wrongright",
         "text": [
            {
               "text": "<p><h3>Wrong, but Right</h3>When the Sumerians proposed that a few kings had ruled for more than 200,000 years, they wrote something impossible; after all, humans have always lived for roughly the same number of decades. No one has ever ruled for 43,200 years.</p><p>But here is what is remarkable: even without telescopes, fossils, or carbon dating, they intuited something profound - humanity is ancient. Their numbers were wrong, but their intuition was correct.</p><p>Today, with scientific evidence, we know that several human species existed over millions of years, and that possibly others are still to be discovered (<htcite0> ; <htcite1> ; <htcite2>).</p>",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "7fa66cfd-dca8-4a17-bead-e79954ba684a",
                     "text": "Wood, B., & Collard, M.",
                     "page": "pp. 65",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "1999",
                        "month": "",
                        "day": ""
                     }
                  },
