      },
      {
         "id": "SECTION_lifeuniverse",
         "text": [
            {
               "text": "<p><h3>Life and the Universe</h3>We have seen numbers hidden in our bodies, in texts, and in ancient king lists. Now let us zoom out - way out - to the largest numbers humanity has ever measured.</p><p>When we talk about the creation of planets and stars, the numbers become enormous. Our current measurements show that the universe is more than 13 billion years old. To put that in perspective: if the entire history of the universe were compressed into a single year, all of recorded human history would occupy less than the last second of December 31. Planet Earth, at less than 5 billion years old, would appear only in mid-September (<htcite0> ; <htcite1>).</p>",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "a1a7c478-9061-4160-ba43-301c081733e9",
                     "text": "©ESA – C. Carreau",
                     "page": "History of Cosmic Formation",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2024",
                        "month": "",
                        "day": ""
                     }
                  },
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "7751f88b-cf1e-466f-ab7f-52fb6a616f5e",
                     "text": "History Tracers Atlas",
                     "page": "The Origin of the Universe",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2024",
                        "month": "",
                        "day": ""
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<p class=\"desc\"><img id=\"imgUniverse\" onclick=\"htImageZoom('imgUniverse', '0%')\" class=\"imgcenter\" style=\"width: 80%;\"/><b>Figure 6</b>: Evolution of the universe, according to the European Space Agency (ESA) (<htcite0>).</p>",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "a1a7c478-9061-4160-ba43-301c081733e9",
                     "text": "©ESA – C. Carreau",
                     "page": "History of Cosmic Formation",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2024",
                        "month": "",
                        "day": ""
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            }
         ]
      },
      {
         "id": "SECTION_universesize",
         "text": [
            {
               "text": "### The Size of the Universe\n\nIt is not only the age of the universe that is gigantic. When we look at its dimensions, we also see other staggering quantities. For example, let us reflect on the stars - those bright points of light you see on a clear night are just a tiny fraction of what is really out there.",
               "source": [],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<p><center><div class=\"first_steps_reflection_parent\"><div class=\"first_steps_reflection question_mark\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-brain\"></i></div><div class=\"first_steps_reflection\"><b>Reflection 2: The universe has...</b><br /><br />How many stars does the universe have?</div></div></center></p>",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<p class=\"desc\"><img id=\"imgGaiaStarFormation\" onclick=\"htImageZoom('imgGaiaStarFormation', '0%')\" src=\"\" class=\"imgcenter\"/><b>Figure 7</b>: Star-forming regions in the Milky Way (<htcite0>).</p>",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "49878bec-a9fb-40b3-9421-cc86648e7a45",
                     "text": "ESA/Gaia/DPAC, S. Payne-Wardenaar, L. McCallum et al",
                     "page": "",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2025",
                        "month": "",
                        "day": ""
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "When we talk about the number of stars in the universe, we enter territory of numbers not yet explored in History Tracers. Moreover, we will no longer talk about an exact number - we will estimate, and that is perfectly fine. Science often works with estimates when exact counting is impossible.\n\nThe first step is to look at our cosmic neighborhood. We live in a vast region of the universe called the Milky Way. In it alone, we have about 100 billion stars - that is, 100,000,000,000 (<htcite0>). If you counted one star per second, non-stop, it would take you over 3,000 years to finish.\n\nThis value of 100 billion stars is just one order of magnitude - one numerical family - larger than the age of the universe, but it only counts the stars in our galaxy. Now, scientists estimate that there are also about 100 billion galaxies in the observable universe (<htcite1>). That is like counting all the grains of sand on every beach on Earth - and then some.\n\nMultiplying 100 billion stars by 100 billion galaxies, we arrive at a staggering number: roughly 1 septillion stars, that is, 1,000,000,000,000,000,000,000,000 (<htcite2>). That is a 1 followed by 24 zeros - more stars than all the grains of sand on every beach and desert on Earth combined.",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 0,
                     "uuid": "ad06d072-85a8-4a96-a31b-88a1e08cafd6",
                     "text": "ESA. How many stars are there in the Universe?",
                     "page": "",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "",
                        "month": "",
                        "day": ""
                     }
                  },
                  {
                     "type": 0,
                     "uuid": "ad06d072-85a8-4a96-a31b-88a1e08cafd6",
                     "text": "ESA. How many stars are there in the Universe?",
                     "page": "",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "",
                        "month": "",
                        "day": ""
                     }
                  },
                  {
                     "type": 0,
                     "uuid": "ad06d072-85a8-4a96-a31b-88a1e08cafd6",
                     "text": "ESA. How many stars are there in the Universe?",
                     "page": "",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "",
                        "month": "",
                        "day": ""
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            }
         ]
      },
      {
         "id": "SECTION_avogrado",
         "text": [
            {
               "text": "### What about inside us?\n\nWe have seen enormous numbers in the age of the universe and the count of stars. But surprisingly, enormous numbers also exist inside us - at the tiniest scale imaginable.\n\nWhen we begin to deepen our knowledge about how nature builds what we see and what we do not see, we discover that inside everything - your hand, a leaf, a drop of water - there are incredibly small elements called atoms. Atoms are so small that a single grain of sand contains more atoms than there are grains of sand on all of Earth's beaches. And inside atoms, scientists found even smaller fundamental particles through experiments that smash particles together at nearly the speed of light (Figure 8) (<htcite0>).",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "2fd2e872-c5d8-4f1b-91dc-97df40597c4e",
                     "text": "History Tracers Team",
                     "page": "Evolution of Knowledge: The Atom",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2025",
                        "month": "",
                        "day": ""
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<p class=\"desc\"><img id=\"imgALICE\"  class=\"imgcenter\" onclick=\"htImageZoom('imgALICE', '0%')\" /><b>Figure 8</b>: Particles produced during a proton collision. © CERN (<htcite0>).</p>",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "1a21963e-5a89-4c1d-8448-e8e80f913f76",
                     "text": "Jacquemod, Melissa Marie",
                     "page": "CERN",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2017",
                        "month": "4",
                        "day": "24"
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "Even before scientists knew about all the particles we know today, a chemist named Amedeo Avogadro made a brilliant prediction: gases under the same conditions would contain the same number of particles in the same volume (<htcite0>).\n\nHis reasoning was correct, but the exact number took decades to determine. Today we call it Avogadro's number: one mole - the unit chemists use to count particles - contains exactly 602,214,076,000,000,000,000,000 particles. That is about 602 sextillion. To grasp this number: a mole of grains of sand would cover the entire surface of Earth to a depth of several meters.",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 0,
                     "uuid": "59784588-19c7-46c1-9abd-9ac2daca5630",
                     "text": "Britannica Editors",
                     "page": "mole",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2026",
                        "month": "08",
                        "day": "13"
                     }
                  },
                  {
                     "type": 0,
                     "uuid": "59784588-19c7-46c1-9abd-9ac2daca5630",
                     "text": "Britannica Editors",
                     "page": "mole",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2026",
                        "month": "08",
                        "day": "13"
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": [],
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            }
         ]
      },
      {
         "id": "SECTION_conclusion",
         "text": [
            {
               "text": "<p><h3>Conclusion</h3>In this lesson, we traveled from the smallest to the largest numbers we know. We saw that numbers are hiding everywhere: in the parts of our body, in the words we read, in ancient king lists, in the age of the universe, in the count of stars, and even in the atoms that make up our bodies.</p><p>We also learned an important lesson about knowledge itself. The Sumerians were wrong about kings living for thousands of years, but they were right that humanity is ancient. Today, thanks to scientific evidence, we can distinguish between poetic stories and proven facts.</p><p>The journey with numbers is just beginning. As you continue studying, you will discover that mathematics is not just a school subject - it is the key to understanding everything around you.</p>",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            }
         ]
      }
